    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d372">

                <pb facs="0325" n="319" xml:id="pg319"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1123 Nyhus, Phoebe/85: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-04-22T13:00:00-05:00">April 22, 1939—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>127. Your 282, April 15, 1 p.m. and Department’s telegram of this date to
                        Chungking<anchor n="53" xml:id="d372-f1"/> regarding Tungpeh bombing which
                    is being repeated to you for information. It is suggested that you inform the
                    Japanese Consul General that as a result of the inaccessibility of the place
                    where Nyhus family is located and difficulties encountered in communicating with
                    them, complete information regarding the extent and seriousness of wounds
                    sustained by Mrs. Nyhus and Ruth Nyhus has not yet been received, that steps are
                    being taken to expedite the matter, and that if he is authorized to make
                    settlement of the claims arising out of the bombing you will furnish him with
                    all obtainable information in due course.</p>
                <p>Further instructions will be sent you upon receipt of information being requested
                    from Chungking and from Board of Missions at Grand Forks.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
